a stroke occurs when the blood flow in  part of your brain is blocked  after just a few minutes the starved  brain cells begin to die  normally the brain receives blood via  two major pairs of arteries  which branched throughout brain tissue  and supply your brain cells with a  constant flow of oxygen glucose and  nutrients necessary for their functions  in one type of stroke ischemic stroke  an artery in your brain Narrows  becomes completely blocked preventing  normal blood flow  blockage may be caused by a blood clot  also called a thrombus which forms in an  unhealthy artery of the brain  the lack of blood flow causes the tissue  the artery supplies to become starved or  ischemic  similarly the blockage may be due to an  embolus  a blood clot that forms elsewhere in the  body  travels to the brain  the embolus lodges in a narrowed artery  and obstructs blood flow  you  in contrast during a hemorrhagic stroke  abnormal bleeding disrupts normal blood  flow  for example in an intracerebral  hemorrhage Extro k--  vessel bursts spilling blood directly  onto your brain while robbing the  intended tissue of nourishment  both the hemorrhage and ischemia destroy  brain tissue  a subarachnoid hemorrhage Extro k--  occurs when a weak spot in a blood  vessel wall called an aneurysm bursts  and leaks blood into the tight space  between your brain and your skull the  high pressure bleeding results in  serious damage to brain tissue  immediate treatment for your stroke may  help to minimize brain cell injury and  death  if you have an ischemic stroke  you may be given medication to break up  the clot causing your stroke  later your doctor may recommend surgery  such as carotid endarterectomy to reduce  your risk of having another ischemic  stroke  in the case of hemorrhagic stroke  emergency surgery may be necessary  to repair damaged arteries  or reduce the pressure of the blood on  your brain  you may be given medication to help the  brains blood flow return to normal
 

